WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.210
Today, I want to talk about reading,
which I consider to be the killer app.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:10.140
It is the killer app for so many things,
for education, for communicating with

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.179
people, for connecting with people
in history, people in different

00:00:14.179 --> 00:00:17.339
countries, and for acquiring languages.

00:00:17.769 --> 00:00:24.249
So, today I want to give you my six tips
on powering up your reading capability,

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:26.929
both for language learning and in general.

00:00:27.259 --> 00:00:29.669
First tip, read a lot.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.539
There is no shortcut.

00:00:31.669 --> 00:00:34.280
People who read a lot become good readers.

00:00:34.519 --> 00:00:37.680
They also become more proficient
in the languages they're learning.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.570
They become better writers
in their own language.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:42.140
Read a lot.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:44.360
Recently I was interested
in the subject of reading.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:49.830
So I Googled different podcasts
on reading, on literacy,

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.050
in different languages.

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:54.939
Along the way, I discovered this
series of lectures from France,

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:56.589
from the Collège de France.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:00.410
And there they described, I think, in
very simple terms, what reading is.

00:01:00.539 --> 00:01:06.670
Reading basically piggybacks on the
areas of the brain that process.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:12.040
Audio meaning sounds come into the
brain and a certain area and the sounds

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.330
become meaning as we become familiar
or are familiar with the language.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.130
And reading has a separate entry port.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:22.970
Initially you go to the sort
of sound area of the brain to

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:24.800
follow the chain down to meaning.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:28.760
And as we become more proficient
readers, we can actually bypass

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:31.760
that and go straight to meaning
at least some of the time.

00:01:31.899 --> 00:01:34.629
Now, not all languages are equal.

00:01:34.905 --> 00:01:38.525
Some languages are more,
call it transparent.

00:01:38.555 --> 00:01:41.525
The relationship between the
letters and the sound is more

00:01:41.525 --> 00:01:43.575
consistent than in other languages.

00:01:43.664 --> 00:01:47.745
However, the areas in the brain
that are activated are all the same.

00:01:47.945 --> 00:01:51.744
And in all cases, the sort of simple
view of reading, which is what I

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:54.705
described, is a process of self education.

00:01:54.705 --> 00:01:58.125
The more we read, the more we
become familiar with what the

00:01:58.125 --> 00:02:00.235
letters represent, the better we do.

00:02:00.285 --> 00:02:02.575
And so if we read a book
like Harry Potter and

00:02:02.895 --> 00:02:10.214
The Sorcerer's Stone, we have 76, 000
opportunities to refine and confirm

00:02:10.264 --> 00:02:15.184
our understanding of how the writing
system connects to sound and then to

00:02:15.194 --> 00:02:20.174
meaning, a magic thing that mankind
didn't have for most of history.

00:02:20.565 --> 00:02:24.645
Now, the educators were not content
with the simple view of reading, this

00:02:24.645 --> 00:02:27.125
sort of magic self educating process.

00:02:27.545 --> 00:02:31.504
And so they came up with
additional sort of details.

00:02:31.515 --> 00:02:36.074
One was this Scarborough's Rope,
which introduces the importance of

00:02:36.074 --> 00:02:41.024
comprehension, the importance of prior
knowledge, the importance of vocabulary.

00:02:41.385 --> 00:02:45.105
But all of those things come
automatically if we do enough reading.

00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:47.025
And listening, which I will get to.

00:02:47.465 --> 00:02:50.204
That's why now I come to my point.

00:02:50.215 --> 00:02:53.855
Number two, don't worry too
much about comprehension.

00:02:54.244 --> 00:02:59.344
If you are reading and you understand 60,
70%, you are still practicing reading.

00:02:59.605 --> 00:03:02.164
You are encountering the
words in different contexts.

00:03:02.584 --> 00:03:06.635
The things that you don't understand
now, you may understand later just

00:03:06.645 --> 00:03:07.915
through the process of reading.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:12.050
And I always like to quote Rubem
Alves, the great Brazilian educator,

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:17.190
who says nothing disrupts the pleasure
of reading as much as being asked

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:18.869
questions about what we are reading.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:24.049
If we follow rule number one and
read enough, we will eventually

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:25.799
understand more and more.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.280
And I will get to the issue of people
with reading difficulties like dyslexia.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.820
So don't fuss about what
you don't understand now.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.609
What matters is how much
will you understand later on.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:36.700
Number three.

00:03:37.055 --> 00:03:42.375
Listen, it's all input, reading, listening
is part of the same process, follows the

00:03:42.375 --> 00:03:46.465
same parts of our brain, except that the
reading comes through a different port.

00:03:46.485 --> 00:03:48.675
So you need to take
advantage of listening.

00:03:48.735 --> 00:03:51.814
I find that if I'm reading something
that's difficult in another

00:03:51.815 --> 00:03:53.615
language, if I can hear it first.

00:03:53.725 --> 00:03:55.325
Then I have a bit of a headstart.

00:03:55.365 --> 00:03:58.125
I have some sense of what
the reading is about.

00:03:58.245 --> 00:03:59.765
Imperfect, you know, 20%.

00:03:59.835 --> 00:04:02.795
I even got a bit of a rhythm,
some momentum on reading.

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:04.185
All of this helps me to read.

00:04:04.284 --> 00:04:07.484
After having read something, if I
then listen to it again, it helps to

00:04:07.484 --> 00:04:11.545
reinforce it because the words that we
notice while listening are also words

00:04:11.545 --> 00:04:13.424
that we will notice better when reading.

00:04:13.564 --> 00:04:17.004
So we should always
incorporate audio with text.

00:04:17.025 --> 00:04:18.345
That's the way LingQ is built.

00:04:18.355 --> 00:04:20.715
That's my basic principle
of language learning.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:26.530
I always have audio and text until
I'm quite advanced in the language.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:30.150
Now, in today's day and
age, we have text to speech.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:34.270
So even while reading, if I'm not sure
how something is pronounced, I can

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:36.080
use text to speech so I can hear it.

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:38.279
So that's another example of how we use...

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.880
Use audio in conjunction with reading.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:42.060
This has never been easier.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:43.900
We have automatic intelligence.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:48.000
So even if you bring in a text which
has no audio, you can convert it to

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:50.970
audio using artificial intelligence.

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:56.380
So use audio to accelerate the
process of learning through.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:04.390
Input and of connecting the
code, the letter code to the,

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:05.800
uh, sound and the meaning.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:08.300
So the third thing is use audio.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:14.310
The fourth thing is very context, read
online, read on paper, read online

00:05:14.359 --> 00:05:18.780
in order to take advantage of online
dictionaries, the audio functions that

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:24.170
are available online, the ability to find
material online, I can read on paper.

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:26.359
And I think it's a good
idea to read on paper.

00:05:26.664 --> 00:05:30.485
In addition to reading online, but
I find it at my current stage in

00:05:30.485 --> 00:05:32.255
Arabic and Persian too difficult.

00:05:32.395 --> 00:05:36.275
On the other hand, in other languages,
Romanian, even Greek, Russian,

00:05:36.284 --> 00:05:38.455
Ukrainian, I can read paper books.

00:05:38.474 --> 00:05:42.645
And I like to vary that content online
and paper, even books in English.

00:05:42.664 --> 00:05:45.575
For example, the professor whom
I mentioned at the beginning,

00:05:45.575 --> 00:05:49.435
Stanislas Duen, I saw him online,
but I also bought the book.

00:05:49.675 --> 00:05:52.995
I've mentioned before Daniel
Willingham, Outsmart Your Brain,

00:05:53.005 --> 00:05:54.235
but I also ended up buying the book.

00:05:54.274 --> 00:05:55.544
So I think it's important.

00:05:55.770 --> 00:06:00.610
In reading to use the advantages of online
learning, but it's also a good thing to

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:04.770
read paper books and it's a good thing
to read a variety of sources on the

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:08.930
same subject rather than trying to beat
yourself up in trying to remember this

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:10.790
word or trying to remember this date.

00:06:10.829 --> 00:06:14.799
If you read two or three different books
on the same subject, you will acquire that

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:18.780
greater knowledge of the subject matter
that will improve your comprehension.

00:06:19.105 --> 00:06:22.605
And comprehension is something that
builds gradually and something that

00:06:22.725 --> 00:06:26.915
personally, I don't like being tested
on using comprehension questions.

00:06:26.984 --> 00:06:30.544
Number five is use the functionality
that's available in different

00:06:30.785 --> 00:06:32.374
language learning apps like LingQ.

00:06:32.435 --> 00:06:34.494
So at LingQ, I very much recommend.

00:06:34.715 --> 00:06:38.625
Sentence view, because there are
always sentences that we didn't quite

00:06:38.685 --> 00:06:41.305
understand, even if we know all the words.

00:06:41.355 --> 00:06:45.115
And I don't like going to sentence
view for every sentence in a text,

00:06:45.115 --> 00:06:48.725
but I do go for those sentences
where I'm having a little difficulty.

00:06:48.775 --> 00:06:51.755
I can group certain words
together as phrases.

00:06:51.764 --> 00:06:55.214
Some words belong with other words
and it's not obvious the first time.

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:57.835
But if I go back into
sentence view, I look at it.

00:06:58.065 --> 00:06:59.305
I can save a phrase.

00:06:59.365 --> 00:07:02.955
I can also do that little exercise
where the sentence gets scrambled and

00:07:02.955 --> 00:07:04.445
I have to put it back together again.

00:07:04.535 --> 00:07:07.175
And I've always said this, you
know, you need both the top down

00:07:07.175 --> 00:07:08.995
learning and the bottom up learning.

00:07:09.045 --> 00:07:11.285
You need the big picture and the details.

00:07:11.325 --> 00:07:13.395
And the sentence view
is a great place to go.

00:07:13.625 --> 00:07:18.805
To get at the details, the DNA of the
language, and that gives you more sort

00:07:18.805 --> 00:07:23.315
of ammunition as you charge forward in
your big picture, listening and reading.

00:07:23.585 --> 00:07:27.815
And finally, number six, I said, I would
talk a bit about dyslexia through these

00:07:27.815 --> 00:07:29.545
different podcasts that I listened to.

00:07:29.685 --> 00:07:33.465
I became aware of a person called
Cliff Weitzman, who developed

00:07:33.535 --> 00:07:37.755
a software called Speechify,
which enables you to convert any.

00:07:38.015 --> 00:07:41.895
Text into audio that's not so unique
nowadays, and he can then increase

00:07:41.895 --> 00:07:43.435
the speed two times, three times.

00:07:43.785 --> 00:07:48.394
And the reason he did that was because
he was dyslexic and he found that

00:07:48.395 --> 00:07:54.895
listening to audio at two or three times
the speed helped him cure his dyslexia.

00:07:54.974 --> 00:07:55.895
Can't vouch for that.

00:07:55.914 --> 00:08:00.745
I don't know whether it's true or not,
but I have been practicing that at LingQ.

00:08:00.805 --> 00:08:05.044
So I can go to the, what we
call the karaoke mode and

00:08:05.409 --> 00:08:06.170
crank it up to 1.5

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:11.810
times, two times, and training
yourself to follow along and to

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:14.530
read at that speed does wonders.

00:08:14.539 --> 00:08:18.989
You actually have more of a bird's
eye view, it seems to me, of the text.

00:08:19.099 --> 00:08:24.020
As you are listening to the new words come
at you rather quickly, you're able to kind

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:26.609
of remember what you just heard earlier.

00:08:26.679 --> 00:08:28.340
It's just a different experience.

00:08:28.465 --> 00:08:32.664
Now, speed listening to a whole audio
book, you're going to miss a lot.

00:08:32.935 --> 00:08:34.465
So that's not the suggestion.

00:08:34.555 --> 00:08:38.524
The suggestion I'm making here is
experiment with different speeds of

00:08:38.525 --> 00:08:43.235
listening to increase your ability
to process the language, not because

00:08:43.244 --> 00:08:45.915
while you're listening at double the
speed, you're going to understand more.

00:08:46.065 --> 00:08:49.915
You'll probably understand less, but I
find that when I go back to listening at

00:08:49.915 --> 00:08:54.225
a normal speed, and even in terms of the
impact on my reading, it's beneficial,

00:08:54.385 --> 00:08:56.105
so it's a bit like this fart leg.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:09:01.720
run for runners, where they experiment
with sprinting and then running at more

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:03.860
of a jogging pace and then sprinting.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:06.990
And apparently this builds
up your aerobic capacity.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:12.239
So my sixth and final suggestion
or tip is play with the speed of

00:09:12.239 --> 00:09:14.359
listening as it connects the reading.

00:09:14.409 --> 00:09:18.169
Build up your capability and reading
increase your enjoyment of reading.

00:09:18.229 --> 00:09:20.979
And I think you will find
benefits, not only in language

00:09:20.979 --> 00:09:22.349
learning, but in life in general.

00:09:22.429 --> 00:09:23.479
And I'll leave you with.

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:26.900
Two videos that I did on the
subject of reading and literacy some

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:30.240
while ago, and you'll see that my
perspective has changed somewhat.

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:31.240
Thanks for listening.

